{"author":{"id":"585844baa6074d44d241f46aa7c9f21ff46653adf617d7f070fbfe327626c989"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1795214307,"nonce":"dRTLrCgXHQMEMCImtoZ4yrsgnk0=","title":"Knowledge publication: field-level updates and relationship diffs in the KyuGraph projection","message":"KyuGraph's deltas merge properties: at the pinned revision (64f41a2), `GraphDelta::UpsertNode` keeps properties the delta does not name, and `UpsertEdge` merges edge properties. The projection in adapters/kyugraph/src/lib.rs does not use that granularity:\n\n- `Knowledge.capture` reads every selected field, and the contract requires a complete selected snapshot per changed entity (docs/knowledge.md). Each node upsert therefore carries every fact plus the `_hxn_*` columns, including facts that did not change.\n- Relationships are replaced wholesale: for each entity in the batch the delta deletes every previous outgoing edge (read from `_hxn_links`) and upserts every current one.\n- Before building the delta, the adapter runs one `MATCH ... WHERE n._hxn_key = $key` per entity to check ownership and read its previous links.\n\nOnly the entities in a batch are written, so publication is already incremental per entity; the cost is per field and per edge. That cost, together with the per-entity queries, is what makes frequent publication (many entities, small changes) expensive, not KyuGraph.\n\nPossible changes, each keeping replay, ownership, revision watermarks and tombstones:\n1. Edge diffs: delete only removed targets and upsert only added ones, from the stored `_hxn_links` already read.\n2. Field-level snapshots: a capture form that selects changed fields (`Knowledge.captureFields(value, [\"goal\"])`, or a dirty set kept by the caller), carried in the batch as a partial snapshot, projected as an UpsertNode naming only those facts. The batch format, MemoryKnowledgeSink semantics and the digest used for replay detection must define what a partial snapshot means, and whether the first publication of an entity must be complete.\n3. Keep each entity's owner and links in the adapter between batches (rebuilt on open and restore) instead of querying them per entity.\n\nThe hard limits stay: eight pending operations per graph, batches of 256 snapshots or 1 MiB, and spatial queries that scan nodes. Per-frame ECS state stays out of the graph either way; this is about cheaper publication at save points, level loads and world events, which hxnme intends to drive from annotated components.","files":null}]}